WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:04.960
Hi, I'm Macey, and I'm here with Colin, and we're going to talk a little bit

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.280
about a new feature that we're shipping called Code

3
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.280
Review.

4
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.160
So what problem are we trying to solve with code review?

5
00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.360
So coding agents are great at writing code,

6
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.520
can write thousands of lines, open thousands of PRs.

7
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.840
Writing code is no longer the bottleneck.

8
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:30.640
However, reviewing it and trusting what it can do is completely the bottleneck.

9
00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.640
Essentially, review is where your accountability sits.

10
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.400
It's kind of the final gate between the code you write, or your agents write,

11
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:43.600
and what gets shipped to the customer who you really want to trust.

12
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.720
That accountability is kind of a key feature built

13
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:49.920
into our code review tool.

14
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.960
So there are a number of kind of third-party tools that do this.

15
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.200
We've used a few.

16
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.320
I'm curious how Tessl's version of code review is different.

17
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.640
So one of the big differentiators with Tessl code review is that it's

18
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:09.040
very aware of the intent of your PR. It reads the summary, the title.

19
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.200
It understands what you are trying to get to with this change.

20
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.720
Some companies might have a real fix-forward philosophy

21
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.080
where they don't want too rigorous reviews and they just want to ship,

22
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.280
whereas other ones might have a very strict set of requirements.

23
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.680
You don't need to tell Tessl code review this.

24
00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.000
It'll know, right?

25
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.040
And it will review accordingly.

26
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:33.120
One issue that we found with existing code

27
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.120
review tools is an issue of trust.

28
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:40.600
You know, they'll find a whole host of issues, but it's really hard to

29
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.440
know if they're true, if they're accurate, and if they match your standards.

30
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:49.720
If you have code review implemented, but you feel you have to review

31
00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.440
that as well, it hasn't really taken anything off your plate.

32
00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.600
So how does Tessl's code review help

33
00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.200
focus review compared to, say, other tools that do something similar?

34
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.200
So we have a concept of review lenses, and these are essentially skills

35
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.640
a conversion you can evaluate, you can distribute

36
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.280
and you can customize them to exactly your repository standards.

37
00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:21.560
You can then scope them using globs to certain sections of your code base.

38
00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:25.480
So you can have a lens specifically for random design, or a lens

39
00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.320
specifically for security.

40
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.920
Tessl code review ships with a really great set of defaults,

41
00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.480
so you don't actually need to configure it to already get value from it.

42
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.960
But I think where the real power comes

43
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.200
is from being able to fine-grain and fine-tune your reviews.

44
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:47.640
So what might someone expect to change if they set up Tessl code review?

45
00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.880
So I think one of the first benefits that people will see when they set up Tessl

46
00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:56.800
code review is they spend a lot less time babysitting a PR to completion.

47
00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:01.000
So as you start customizing and configuring Tessl code review,

48
00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:05.200
you'll find that the knowledge and learnings will compound.

49
00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:08.800
So your reviews will get better, you'll trust them more.

50
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.520
And your agents will be able to work more autonomously in a way that works for you.

51
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.560
So one of its kind of key benefits

52
00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.560
is that you own the standard of review.

53
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.680
It's a file in your repo.

54
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.760
It could be a skill, your configuration file. You own it.

55
00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.520
You can version it. You can share it how you want.

56
00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:33.320
It's not some setting in some web UI of some other code review product.

57
00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.480
It's not some black box where you don't know what's going on and how it runs.

58
00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:42.320
You really own the standard. One thing about being able to version your review lenses

59
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:47.280
is that you can now apply a consistent set of reviews across every repo.

60
00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.800
But not only that, you can obviously account for specific

61
00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:56.320
repo differences with other lenses, but it means you can have the baseline

62
00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:01.280
level of review across your organization, across your code bases.

63
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.800
Obviously, you know, we're pretty deep into factory land here at Tessl.

64
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.720
What's your opinion on kind of where this sits in sort of our big factory plans?

65
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.440
So Tessl code review is a first-class component of a software factory.

66
00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:20.680
Software factories run relatively autonomously.

67
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.000
Ship a lot of code.

68
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.800
You need to be sure that that code is trustworthy,

69
00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.080
and it's a thing that you want it to do.

70
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:34.040
This is why Tessl code review integrates directly into your software factory.

71
00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.320
So how can we get started with Tessl code review?

72
00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.760
The easiest way to get started is getting the Tessl agent,

73
00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:45.760
aim it at your repo and say, hey, set up code review for me,

74
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.120
and it'll set up the workflows, it'll set up all the actions.

75
00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.880
It'll set up your configuration files

76
00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.560
and also help you customize your own review lenses.

77
00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.240
You can also just point your agent of choice at our docs on our website.

78
00:04:58.240 --> 00:04:59.320
At tessl.io

79
00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.000
and ask it to set up code review.

80
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.720
Thank you so much for watching.

81
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:06.720
Thank you for your time.

82
00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.120
If you want to get started with code review, you can do so at tessl.io.

83
00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:13.520
And we'll see you next time.
